WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.440
The first step to becoming a polyglot
is to learn one more language.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.680
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
and today I want to talk about

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.780
how to become a polyglot.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.220
Now, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.720
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.770
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:20.670
I do appreciate it.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.840
So how do we become polyglots?

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.465
Now the reason I'm talking about this is
because, um, my wife and I were playing

00:00:30.645 --> 00:00:34.665
golf the other day at our golf club with
a couple, uh, probably in their sixties.

00:00:35.145 --> 00:00:39.015
And often you, you know, you're, you're
teamed up with people you don't know, this

00:00:39.015 --> 00:00:41.355
was our first chance to meet this couple.

00:00:41.355 --> 00:00:45.375
And they told us they were going on
a bicycle trip with their friends,

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:51.325
eight couples going to Girona in Spain,
uh, to bicycle around in Catalonia.

00:00:51.945 --> 00:00:55.515
So I said, are you going to
learn Catalan or Spanish?

00:00:55.515 --> 00:00:56.995
Of course, I was going
to push LingQ, you know.

00:00:57.605 --> 00:00:59.970
So, which I did of
course, but to no avail.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.470
Uh, and so I said, are you gonna
learn Catalan or Spanish or Spanish?

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.259
And the answer was, well, I've heard
that you can get by with English.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.660
All right, true enough.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.070
Perhaps, maybe, maybe not.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:11.730
I don't know.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.350
In the city of Girona, uh, certainly
at the hotel and the restaurants you

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.675
can get by in the shops, but then,
um, outside of Girona I don't know.

00:01:21.705 --> 00:01:25.215
I think if you're in a little town and
you get a flat tire and you're in trouble,

00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:27.105
uh, maybe English is not so common.

00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:28.665
I simply, I just don't know.

00:01:28.935 --> 00:01:35.304
But for me, if I were going on a bicycle
trip to Catalonia, I would learn Catalan.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:39.345
Now I speak Spanish, but so I
have the mentality of a polyglot

00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:40.605
to me that would be fun.

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:44.625
Fun to explore Catalan, if I
learned Catalan, I would read

00:01:44.625 --> 00:01:46.545
books about Catalonia in Catalan.

00:01:46.875 --> 00:01:49.145
I would speak to people in Catalan.

00:01:49.695 --> 00:01:51.915
I, that gives me a
great sense of pleasure.

00:01:51.915 --> 00:01:55.785
I, I feel that I'm enriching my life by
learning another language, connecting

00:01:55.785 --> 00:01:56.805
with people in their language.

00:01:57.765 --> 00:01:59.805
I have the mentality of a polyglot.

00:02:00.405 --> 00:02:05.445
Uh, I tweeted about this and I called this
the English language disease, that English

00:02:05.445 --> 00:02:10.185
speakers, they have the option, or they
think they have the option of not learning

00:02:10.185 --> 00:02:12.175
the language because we can get by.

00:02:13.855 --> 00:02:19.495
And most of the responses to my
tweet, we're very much in agreement.

00:02:19.855 --> 00:02:21.685
Gee, that's too bad, blah, blah, blah.

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:24.805
But one fellow from England,
or maybe he was somewhere else.

00:02:24.805 --> 00:02:29.225
But his, his description showed that
he's from Manchester, he says where I

00:02:29.225 --> 00:02:33.025
live, there was just a Portuguese person
showing a Danish person around our

00:02:33.355 --> 00:02:35.155
compound and they were speaking English.

00:02:35.185 --> 00:02:36.445
That's just the reality.

00:02:36.445 --> 00:02:37.555
That's how it works.

00:02:37.555 --> 00:02:38.125
Too bad.

00:02:38.365 --> 00:02:41.435
So that person also doesn't have
the mentality of a polyglot.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.220
So number one, to become a polyglot,
you have to want to become a polyglot.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.030
You have to like speaking languages,
not just one, but several.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.890
So that is a mindset.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:53.370
Okay.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.320
The second thing is, you know, I
didn't set out to become a polyglot.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.740
I had not really heard the
term polyglot in English.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.880
To me, a linguist was someone who
spoke several languages, but I also

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.770
didn't set out to become a linguist.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.780
I got interested in French.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.520
And then I had the opportunity to
learn Mandarin and then I was in

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.770
Japan, so I learned Japanese on my own.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.060
And stepping back when I was in
France, I would hitchhike around

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.195
in Europe and I would always try
to speak uh, the local language.

00:03:24.285 --> 00:03:30.795
So I think what I find, what I think
is useful to note there is that the

00:03:30.795 --> 00:03:35.055
first step to becoming a polyglot
is to learn one more language.

00:03:35.265 --> 00:03:35.745
Okay.

00:03:36.105 --> 00:03:41.325
Because for people who have never really
acquired fluency in another language,

00:03:41.475 --> 00:03:45.225
who have never converted themselves
into a fluent speaker of another

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:47.984
language, they don't have that feeling.

00:03:47.984 --> 00:03:48.565
So the...

00:03:48.864 --> 00:03:52.320
you can't, sort of skip past
the stage of learning one

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.640
language to become a polyglot.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.200
You learn one language.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.350
You now feel confident that
you can learn languages.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.020
You enjoy the process, or maybe you don't.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.950
Um, I think if you're doing it
the right way, you can enjoy it.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.520
And then what happened to me
and I think what would happen

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.230
to a lot of people is you...

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.579
"gee, I learned one language.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.620
Now I'd like to learn another language."

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.430
Because the experience of speaking
another language, of connecting with

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.329
people in their language is so rewarding
that we want to do it again and again,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.680
and that sense of satisfaction that
actually we were able to succeed

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.990
in, in this task, which is not such
an easy task, uh, but that sense of

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.810
satisfaction makes us want to do it again.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.710
Now, another thing about
becoming a polyglot, in my

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.695
opinion, we shouldn't be afraid.

00:04:40.724 --> 00:04:45.284
Obviously, when we go from language
now B to language C and then

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:49.724
language D that means that we
aren't spending time on language B.

00:04:50.025 --> 00:04:53.085
So we're leaving language B
because I firmly believe we

00:04:53.085 --> 00:04:54.765
never master another language.

00:04:55.005 --> 00:04:57.344
We can always improve
in that other language.

00:04:57.675 --> 00:05:02.145
And yet we make the decision, I'm
not going to become even better.

00:05:02.175 --> 00:05:04.725
Let's say my best foreign
language is French.

00:05:04.815 --> 00:05:07.115
So I'm not going to become
even better in French.

00:05:07.865 --> 00:05:10.215
Or let's say that the
next language is Japanese.

00:05:10.425 --> 00:05:13.125
And I know there are great
gaps in my Japanese learning.

00:05:13.455 --> 00:05:13.695
Okay.

00:05:13.695 --> 00:05:16.605
I'm not going to work on
becoming even better in Japanese.

00:05:17.505 --> 00:05:19.815
I'm going to go and
explore a new language.

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:20.955
So there's a choice there.

00:05:20.955 --> 00:05:25.905
You sacrifice sort of what I would
consider the futile pursuit of perfection.

00:05:25.905 --> 00:05:29.745
Now other people can disagree and they
want to pursue perfection, that's fine.

00:05:29.745 --> 00:05:33.225
I'm not sort of making any value
judgements, but I think to be a

00:05:33.325 --> 00:05:38.355
polygloy, it's different than trying
to become perfect in one language.

00:05:38.715 --> 00:05:42.855
So we accept the fact that we're
going to not be as good in some of

00:05:42.855 --> 00:05:44.355
the languages that we have learned.

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:49.575
We even accept the fact that there's going
to be interference, not only from our

00:05:49.575 --> 00:05:53.535
own language, which is always the case,
but there's going to be interference from

00:05:53.535 --> 00:05:57.855
these languages that we have learned in
the new languages that we are trying to

00:05:57.855 --> 00:06:03.045
learn, because I find myself sometimes
when I'm searching for words in Russian

00:06:03.045 --> 00:06:06.195
the other day I hadn't spoken Russian for
a long time and I was speaking Russian I'm

00:06:06.195 --> 00:06:08.805
searching for words and up comes Persian.

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:13.215
So the more languages you have
in your head bouncing around as

00:06:13.215 --> 00:06:17.805
your brain is going down different
pathways to find words there greater

00:06:17.805 --> 00:06:22.155
likelihood is that it's going to go
into some rabbit hole, as they say.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.830
Uh, where you're into
an yet another language.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.880
So I think part of becoming a
polyglot is accepting that you're

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.260
going to be less than perfect.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.840
That means you're going
to speak with mistakes.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:39.450
Your pronunciation may not be perfect,
but you have this, this, um, sense of

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:46.050
achievement and the, the, uh, intellectual
stimulus of discovering a new world

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:51.570
yet another new world with all of
the people, and as you learn another

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:55.320
language, then the people of that language
come alive, the history comes alive.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.680
And so I think the secret to being a
polyglot is to be interested in many

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.120
different languages and cultures.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:09.450
Um, it may be that some people are
motivated to learn languages in order

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:13.620
to demonstrate that they they're
able to learn languages and, and you

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.390
know, like another notch on my belt.

00:07:15.479 --> 00:07:18.020
I only had eight
languages now I have nine.

00:07:18.495 --> 00:07:22.995
And that can also be a valid, um,
you know, motivation for becoming

00:07:22.995 --> 00:07:26.775
a polyglot, just to demonstrate
your prowess at learning languages.

00:07:27.345 --> 00:07:31.635
Uh, I wonder if that can sustain
you though, because if all

00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:32.565
you want to do is show off.

00:07:32.565 --> 00:07:34.545
Look, I can say a few things
in this other language.

00:07:34.845 --> 00:07:39.495
That doesn't take a lot of, of
time and effort and you can get to

00:07:39.495 --> 00:07:42.415
where you can say hello, how are
you in many different languages.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.720
And, uh, that too is fun.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:50.430
I'm not criticizing that, but in so
far as my own motivation as a polyglot,

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.910
if you look at my experience, not
with Persian and Arabic, which are

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:58.110
my 19th and 20th languages, I've
been at them for more than two years.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.050
It's not a, it's not like three months.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:05.430
And, uh, I'm continuing to
discover new things about those

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.790
languages in those cultures.

00:08:07.245 --> 00:08:12.825
And I do want to get back to Turkish
and I hear that we have, we may have

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:18.315
our LingQ mini stories done up in Urdu,
in which case, once we get LingQ 5.0

00:08:18.315 --> 00:08:23.745
launched, we will add Urdu and then
I would like to get into Urdu as my

00:08:23.805 --> 00:08:28.395
approach into the south Asian languages,
because then because I'm a bit lazy at

00:08:28.395 --> 00:08:32.475
learning new writing systems, then I
won't have to, you know, learn either

00:08:32.475 --> 00:08:35.085
Hindi or Punjabi writing systems.

00:08:35.085 --> 00:08:40.664
And I'll still be able to get some sense
of the sounds of, of those languages.

00:08:40.695 --> 00:08:43.544
I may still end up learning
the writing system, we'll see.

00:08:43.905 --> 00:08:49.814
But you know this again, as a
polyglot, I have this tremendous

00:08:49.814 --> 00:08:53.985
world of languages to look forward
to, and I don't know which languages

00:08:53.985 --> 00:08:55.525
I'm going to explore in the future.

00:08:55.955 --> 00:08:58.770
Uh, I think we'll have Tagalog.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:00.610
We'll have Vietnamese at LingQ.

00:09:00.689 --> 00:09:04.770
Thai, maybe, uh, maybe Indonesian,
maybe Swahili something

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:05.970
on the African continent.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:06.839
I don't know.

00:09:06.959 --> 00:09:10.890
But as a polyglot, my motivation
is to get in there and explore

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.280
these, but I recognize that each
and every one of them is going to

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.339
be a lot of work, uh, but enjoyable.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.890
And, but for me, that's fine.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.060
That's what I have to look forward to.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.420
So perhaps that's my perspective
on how to become a polyglot.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.970
But to summarize, you have to
be motivated to learn languages.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.220
You have to be interested
in learning these languages.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.130
You have to be interested
in a particular language.

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.600
You learn them one at a time.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:40.290
And as you achieve success in one
language, you may find that you're

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.570
motivated to learn another language.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:48.240
You have to be unafraid of
interference from languages that

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:49.920
you have previously learned.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.400
You have to be unafraid of the
fact that some of the languages

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.670
that you have learned will
actually get very, very rusty.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:59.520
You may in fact, not be able
to speak in that language.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.090
Like if you were to ask me now
to speak in Romanian or Greek, I

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.500
would, I wouldn't be able to do it.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:08.640
And yet I know that I have learned
those languages to a level where I

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.300
went to the country and I was able to
communicate and that's okay because I

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:17.400
can't, you know, if you have 20 pots
on a stove, it's very difficult to keep

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.660
them all, you know, simmering without
burning any one of them or without

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:23.190
the fire going out here and there.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:23.820
So that's fine.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:30.180
So all of those things, I think, uh, are
part of the, the, um, you know, at least

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:34.200
in my case, the attitude of a polyglot,
I can't speak for other polyglots.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:35.790
So there you have it.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.260
How to become a polyglot.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:38.190
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.180
Thank you for listening.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:39.450
Bye.

